WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.830
This video is about a forbidden technique
in Python, the twin snake sacrifice juts-,

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:09.559
storing mutable or non-hashable things in
sets and dictionaries.

00:00:09.559 --> 00:00:13.809
Normal sets and dicts use hashing, meaning
when you want to check if an element is in

00:00:13.809 --> 00:00:17.330
there, you use the element's hash to pick
the exact index where the element would go

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:22.119
if it was present, so you can just check that
one location instead of checking every location.

00:00:22.119 --> 00:00:25.000
It's kind of like when you go to the grocery
store and need to find the milk.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.360
You don't walk through every aisle, you just
go straight to the refrigerated section and

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:32.329
if it's not there, then they're out, no need
to check the rest of the store.

00:00:32.329 --> 00:00:36.170
But hashes are necessarily based on the state
of an object in order to make hashes be different

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:38.640
for as many different objects as possible.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.980
Hash collisions are allowed, they just degrade
performance.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:45.730
Like if the grocery store made the whole store
refrigerated then you would have to fall back

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:48.219
to searching every aisle to find the milk.

00:00:48.219 --> 00:00:51.640
But if hashes are based on object state, then
mutating the object would probably change

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:55.109
its hash, so if you went to go find it later,
you'd look in the wrong place.

00:00:55.109 --> 00:00:59.680
For that reason, the usual wisdom is that
you shouldn't make mutable things hashable.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:04.210
Python tries to enforce this by defining a
hash for your classes by default, but not

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:07.159
defining the hash function if you define an
equals function.

00:01:07.159 --> 00:01:12.580
In practice, this is a solid 6/10 solution
but realistically Python can't do any better,

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:16.400
it can't read your mind and there's no default
that would suit every case, so if you have

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.530
an opinion about whether or not your type
should be hashable, then either define the

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:21.700
hash yourself or set it to None.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:26.400
But sometimes you just want a set of mutable
or unhashable things, so what do you do?

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.070
You could use a list or create your own mapping
or set type that stores data in a list, but

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:33.140
this is not the forbidden technique.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:37.290
The problem is looking up elements becomes
incredibly slow because you lose that constant

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:39.570
time lookup that hashing provides.

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:42.720
You're falling back to checking every aisle
in the store for the milk.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:48.960
Instead, let's take advantage of a key property
of every Python object, its identity.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.939
Every Python object, hashable or not, mutable
or not, has an integer id that's guaranteed

00:01:53.939 --> 00:01:57.759
to be both unique and constant for the duration
of its lifetime.

00:01:57.759 --> 00:02:01.040
You can access this id by using the built-in
id function.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:06.049
Id's can be reused, after one object is garbage
collected, its old id could be reassigned

00:02:06.049 --> 00:02:10.900
to a new object, but as long as an object
is alive, the id is an immutable hashable

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:12.879
value that uniquely determines the object.

00:02:12.879 --> 00:02:15.959
I hope you see where I'm going with this.

00:02:15.959 --> 00:02:19.930
So what we're going to do is make our own
mapping and set types that internally never

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:24.680
hash the object itself, but instead hash the
id of the object.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:28.730
In our mapping type, we'll still use a plain
old dict, but we'll map the identity of a

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:30.620
key to its key value pair.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:35.430
Again, the id of a key is just an int, it's
perfectly fine to use as a key of a dictionary,

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:38.730
regardless of whether or not the key itself
is hashable.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:42.120
The result of looking up an id is a key value
pair.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:46.180
Storing the key in addition to the value ensures
the key isn't garbage collected and that the

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:50.980
key's id won't get reused by another object
as long as this mapping exists.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:55.680
Getting an item now looks up the id of the
key up in the dict and returns the value.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:59.180
Setting an item maps the key id to the key
value pair.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:03.150
And deleting an item deletes the id of the
key from the map.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:06.580
There are a few other functions here but they
are pretty straightforward, and we take advantage

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:11.400
of the collections MutableMapping ABC to fill
in the rest of the usual dictionary interface

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.190
like get, pop, and so on.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:18.830
Our set type is similar, we keep a plain old
dict of value ids to values.

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:22.569
Again we're storing the value in addition
to its id to prevent values from being garbage

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:25.430
collected while the set is still alive.

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:29.580
Adding an element to the set means mapping
its id to its value, discarding an element

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:34.299
means popping its id from the map, and checking
if an element is in the set is just checking

00:03:34.299 --> 00:03:37.099
if its id is a key in the dictionary.

00:03:37.099 --> 00:03:41.290
Once again, there are a few other pretty simple
functions and we use collections' MutableSet

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:44.549
abstract base class to fill out the rest of
the usual set interface.

00:03:44.549 --> 00:03:49.209
And there we go, we can now create a list,
which is not hashable, put it in our mapping

00:03:49.209 --> 00:03:54.110
or set no problem, even mutate it in-between,
and we can still look it up no problem.

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:56.709
Mutating the list doesn't change its id.

00:03:56.709 --> 00:03:58.019
Great!

00:03:58.019 --> 00:03:59.990
So what's the downside?

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:05.040
The downside is that when I lookup an element,
I need to use the exact same object, not just

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:06.840
an equal one.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:13.879
Compare to a normal set, if I add 257 in,
then add int(float(257)) in, the set just

00:04:13.879 --> 00:04:17.109
has one element, 257.

00:04:17.109 --> 00:04:21.980
But if I use the IdSet, the same code would
add in two copies of 257, because the two

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:27.610
257s, although equal, are different Python
objects that have different ids.

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:33.669
It is still a set and it does filter duplicates,
but in order to be considered a duplicate,

00:04:33.669 --> 00:04:36.949
you have to add in the exact same object twice.

00:04:36.949 --> 00:04:41.100
This can be especially confusing for small
object types like ints and strings or for

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:45.180
types where it's not clear whether its operations
mutate the object in-place or return a new

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:46.260
one back to you.

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:51.070
I highly discourage using these types with
IdMapping or IdSet, and this potential for

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:54.270
confusion is the reason this is forbidden
Python knowledge.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:58.479
But, if you're certain your objects will be
stored and looked up using the exact same

00:04:58.479 --> 00:05:01.400
object, then you might find this trick to
come in handy.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:05.389
